ORDER NOTE PARSER

Order Note Parser — a real-world task using this week's concepts: split(), strip(), upper(), len(), in, for loops, continue, accumulating totals, comparisons, if / elif / else, and 2-decimal money format. No imports, no tricks.

Solve it individually, then come together as a group to compare approaches. Good luck!

A provision store takes orders as short coded notes - one note per product, in the shape:

item:quantity:unit price

for example: golden morn:2:1750
The owner wants a program that reads exactly 3 notes (use a for loop), prints a receipt line for each good note, and skips bad notes without crashing.

The rules:
- Split each note on the colon. It must come apart into exactly 3 parts - if not, print "Bad note - skipped" and move on to the next note (continue).
- Clean the item part: strip spaces, show in ALL CAPS. Quantity is a whole number, unit price can have decimals (assume both are typed in digits).
- Each good note prints one line: GOLDEN MORN x2 - N3500.00
- Keep a running grand total of the good lines only.
- After the three notes: GRAND TOTAL: N7050.00 (good notes only, two decimal places).

Sample run:

Order note: golden morn : 2 : 1750
GOLDEN MORN x2 - N3500.00
Order note: Milo
Bad note - skipped
Order note::1:500
Bad note - skipped

GRAND TOTAL: N3500.00

Edge cases to handle: extra spaces around every part (still processed), a note with only 2 parts, a note with an empty item but 3 parts (an item that strips down to nothing is a bad note too), a quantity of 0 (line prints with N0.00 and still counts toward the total), and a decimal unit price like 750.50.
